## Concise Matching with if let

`if let` is a shorthand for matching a single pattern. When you only care about one variant (usually `Some`), `if let` is cleaner than a full `match`.

### Basic if let

```xiom
let value: Option[Int] = Some(42);

if let Some(x) = value {
  io.println("Got: " + x);
}
// Only executes if value is Some — and x is bound to the inner value
```

### if let vs match

Match (verbose):
```xiom
match value {
  Some(x) => { io.println("Got: " + x); }
  None => { /* do nothing */ }
}
```

if let (concise):
```xiom
if let Some(x) = value {
  io.println("Got: " + x);
}
```

### if let with else

```xiom
fn describe(opt: Option[Int]) -> Str {
  if let Some(n) = opt {
    return "Value: " + n;
  } else {
    return "Nothing";
  }
}
```

### Early Returns

```xiom
fn process(maybe_data: Option[Str]) -> Int {
  if let Some(data) = maybe_data {
    return data.len();
  }
  return 0;  // None case
}
```

### if let with Multiple Operations

```xiom
if let Some(user) = lookup_user(42) {
  io.println("Welcome, " + user.name);
  io.println("ID: " + user.id);
}
```

